<title>GERMANY: Waigel-Telekom sales won't help EMU deficit.</title>
<headline>Waigel-Telekom sales won't help EMU deficit.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1997-05-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>German Finance Minister Theo Waigel reiterated on Thursday that Bonn planned to sell additional shares in Deutsche Telekom this year, but said this would not help Germany reach the Maastricht deficit target.</p>
<p>Waigel told a news conference in Bonn that the revenues would instead be used to pay off old debt.</p>
<p>&quot;I think it is possible, and I will propose, to bring forward parts of the privatisation to 1997,&quot; he said.  </p>
<p>Waigel on Wednesday proposed bringing forward the sale of more Telekom shares following the successful flotation of an initial tranche of Telekom stock late last year.</p>
<p>Bonn still owns around 75 percent of Telekom, but will have to gain approval from the company to sell more shares. The shares may also not be sold in the open market.</p>
</text>
